### Procedural body timelines

- All20 catalog body roots now use retained source timelines and12 fitted blue leaf approximations, bringing the procedural import to109 sprites,25 shape-bound records and5097 placements. Normal, dark and light bodies retain their distinct source colors and glints. Three Huge Base variants have one authored frame and remain static; the other17 body roots animate.
- The existing shared clip engine now drives mock bodies in gameplay, detail previews, draft placement, placed designs and forcefield silhouettes. This includes40-frame bubble breathing and the actual Wiggle, Air Bladder, Tentacle, Smooth Tentacle and Sliding Cluster transforms. The longest root has340 frames; child phases, root frame-script branches and loop jumps remain independent. No universal scale/opacity pulse substitutes for those movements.
- Procedural body posters and stopped-frame class captures now compose those same fitted leaves and initialized trees. Smooth Tentacle permits a rounded random start at0, which resolves to its empty frame1; empty poses retain zero Script bounds and transparent posters instead of a generic fallback bubble. The creator now preserves that empty initial measurement in both asset modes. Exact contours, antialiasing and original global random/event ordering remain approximations; the pending geometric collision audit above is separate from animation playback.
- Verification:411 rule/parser/data tests with5516 assertions, typecheck, formatting and production build passed. All16 affected browser checks passed: all20 mock/source body trees and their silhouettes at12 ages through400 frames, moving previews and placed clusters in the actual mock editor, the existing31 weapon/behavior references, native/mock placement, body-only class captures, damage and protection clocks/sizing. The three static base bodies stayed static. Both the comparison gallery and actual mock editor were visually inspected; retained captures are `/tmp/bt3-body-motion-comparison.png` and `/tmp/bt3-mock-creator-live-bodies.png`. The research probe was also regenerated and rerun successfully with13 expected results and no browser errors. Preview3100 serves the verified body build. Complete one-to-one game parity remains open.

### Geometric contact and retained native attachments

- `shapeHitGeometry` recovers fill contours and stroke paths from the supplied SWF independently of paint. `ShapeContact` uses Canvas path geometry, authored winding, widths, caps, joins and declared bounds, without raster alpha thresholds, glow or filter expansion. Native static and interpolated morph leaves now use this provider through the existing retained matrices and mask traversal. The cache is bounded at256 entries and is released with the pack. No original paths or artwork are committed. Procedural parts use their fitted circles/rims; the invisible green muzzle helper23 contributes its recovered1.45px radius despite having no paint.
- Hidden enemy/ally bodies remain geometric targets, as do zero-alpha parts and transparent fills/strokes. Actual removed mounts do not. The older image/contact test was corrected to assert visual disappearance while contact remains; healing restores artwork, rather than recreating lost contact. Carrier release also removes its direct-child registry entry, following `_‑BL:262–280`'s `removeChild`, while already attached status marks survive at their chosen positions.
- Native sticky/stun/jam/weaken marks, infection, shield film/hit clips and attached forcefield splashes now participate in whole-tank contact. Forcefield contact is intersected with its independently animated full-design clone mask; that mask does not hit independently. Logical detachment removes participation. Rendering and contact share protection sizing, rotations and stage ages. A retained arrival film can therefore intercept a shot outside the base tank and still allow its damage, because immunity was cleared separately. The production browser fixture verifies both the protected and non-protected contact paths using actual projectile callbacks.
- `infectedbubblepop_398`1737 has a rounded random0…10 start on frame1, blank frames before10, and a frame18→1 loop. The former raw-clock view synchronized all nine children and missed those constructor actions. `AssetPack.infectionClip` now shares a retained tree between native painting and contact, with independent child starts and continued stage time through paused gameplay. Its private stream is repeatable and does not reproduce the original global RNG. Import validation now explicitly covers all8 frame-scripted attachment clips, in addition to the25 part clips; regeneration leaves the existing231 imported action tables unchanged.
- Remaining boundaries: controlled Adobe comparison, exact boundary/twip and unusual stroke semantics, deeper mask/event ordering, independent simultaneous shield-mount callbacks and older turret-hit instances, and full live-child protection sizing. A bounded source scan found no hairline, non-scaling or unequal-cap strokes among the119 static shape leaves reachable from the part/projectile/status/protection roots; arbitrary future artwork may require additional stroke behavior. Mock status-decoration contact silhouettes remain approximate/omitted with their existing procedural paint; mock shields/forcefields use procedural proxies. Full game parity remains unproven.
- Verification:416 rule/parser/data tests with5535 assertions, typecheck, formatting and production build passed. All24 affected browser checks passed, including geometric/transparent/masked contact, cache bounds/disposal, hidden-body damage art, actual shield-film projectile damage, native status marks surviving carrier removal, infection, forcefield clone contact/detachment, native beam/turret fixtures, glow, Whip/Grow/Flak clocks, projectile order, seeker mines, special projectiles, mounted charges and tank/protection clocks. The earlier native attachment fixture required an authored infection pop phase and retained status marks after carrier removal to expose their geometry; those source-backed cases now pass. The native gallery was visually inspected and retained at `/tmp/bt3-native-shape-contact.png`, with the mock counterpart at `/tmp/bt3-mock-shape-contact.png`. These checks validate the recovered contracts, not complete game parity.
test('hidden and alpha-zero tank parts retain contact while removed parts do not', () => {
  const pack = new AssetPack('mock'),
    body = { ...STARTER.parts[0], type: 1001, x: 0, y: 0, scale: 1 };
  Object.assign(pack, {
    leaves: new Map([[679, {
      x: 6, y: 6, width: 1, height: 1, resolution: 1,
      canvas: { width: 1, height: 1 } as HTMLCanvasElement,
      pixels: new Uint8ClampedArray([7, 129, 180, 255]), url: '',
    }]]),
    sprites: new Map([
      [
        partById(1001)!.symbol,
        {
          id: 680,
          frameCount: 1,
          placements: [
            {
              charId: 679,
              depth: 2,
              frame: 1,
              matrix: { a: 1, b: 0, c: 0, d: 1, tx: -130, ty: -130 },
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
    ]),
  });
  const tank = createTank(1, { ...STARTER, parts: [body] }, 'enemy');
  expect(pack.hitTest(tank, 0, 0)).toBe(true);
  tank.hiddenParts.add(body.key);
  tank.alpha = 0;
  expect(pack.hitTest(tank, 0, 0)).toBe(true);
  tank.removedParts.add(body.key);
  expect(pack.hitTest(tank, 0, 0)).toBe(false);
});

